# Highly selective recognition of adenosine 5′-triphosphate against other nucleosides triphosphate with a luminescent metal-organic framework of [Zn(BDC)(H2O)2]n(BDC = 1,4-benzenedicarboxylate)

Highly selective recognition of adenosine ${\pmb5}^{\prime}$ -triphosphate against other nucleosides triphosphate with a luminescent metal-organic framework of $\mathbf{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ ( $\mathbf{\sigma}_{\mathbf{BDC}}=1{,}4$ -benzenedicarboxylate)
1Education Ministry Key Laboratory on Luminescence and Real-Time Analysis; School of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering, Southwest University, Chongqing 400715, China 2College of Pharmaceutical Science, Southwest University, Chongqing 400716, China
Selective recognition of adenosine ${5}^{\prime}$ -triphosphate (ATP) is of great significance owing to its indispensable functions to organisms. Also, it is a challenging task because other nucleosides triphosphate hold the same triphosphate group and structurally planar bases as ATP. It is known that metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a new type of sensing material. In this work, highly selective recognition of ATP against other nucleosides triphosphate is successfully achieved with a luminescent MOF of $\mathrm{\Delta}[Z{\mathrm{n}}(\mathbf{BDC})(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})_{2}]_{n}$ $\mathrm{BDC}^{2-}=1{,}4$ -benzenedicarboxylate). $\mathrm{\Delta}[Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{BDC})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]_{n}$ dispersed in water shows a remarkable redshift of the emission wavelength upon addition of ATP, while cytidine ${5}^{\prime}$ -triphosphate (CTP), uridine $5^{\prime}{\mathrm{.}}$ -triphosphate (UTP), and guanosine $5^{\prime}{\mathrm{.}}$ -triphosphate (GTP), as well as some inorganic anions such as $\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}{}^{4-}$ or $\mathrm{PO_{4}}^{3-}$ can’t induce such spectral change as ATP. $\mathrm{^1H}$ NMR, $^{31}\mathrm{P}$ NMR and Raman spectra indicate that both $\pi\cdot\pi$ stacking interactions and the coordination of $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ with adenine and the phosphate group are involved in the interaction of $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ with ATP. In addition, the experimental results showed that the redshift extent of the emission wavelength of $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ has the linear relationship with the concentration of ATP in the range of $0.3{\mathrm{-}}1.8~{\mathrm{mmol/L}}$ . Based on this, the detection of ATP content in the sample of ATP injection was made with satisfactory results. This system pioneers the application of MOFs in the recognition of nucleotides, and testifies that the participation of base in the recognition process can improve the selectivity against the other nucleotides.
adenosine ${\pmb5}^{\prime}$ -triphosphate, luminescent, metal-organic framework
## 1  Introduction
Recognition of biologically related anions such as various nucleotides has always been an active area of research owing to their important functions in organisms [1–5]. However, anions, especially those nucleosides triphosphate, are known to be pH-sensitive and have larger sizes, and their recognition is not so easy as cations. So, it is challenging to design chemosensors for these anions. Adenosine- ${\boldsymbol{\cdot}}{\boldsymbol{5^{\prime}}}$ -triphosphate (ATP), as a common but very important nucleotide anion, is of special interest because it plays an indispensable role in the energy storage and transfer of all living cells, and can also act as a signaling molecule in regulating the KATP channel [6] and the mammalian target of Rapamycin pathway [7]. Accordingly, great efforts have been taken to develop chemosensors for selective recognition of ATP with fluorescent and colorimetric methods [4–5].
To our knowledge, besides the use of aptamers for ATP recognition and detection [8–11], organic small molecules (OSMs) are also considered as a useful tool. Generally, there are two strategies based on the OSMs for the recognition of ATP. One is the direct use of OSMs, which are functionally designed and prepared by endowing with imidazolium [12, 13], amide [14], polyamine [15], or cyclophane [16] moiety, and thus binding between OSMs and ATP occurs through hydrogen bonding, and electrostatic interactions with phosphate group and $\pi-\pi$ stacking with adenine, respectively (Scheme 1(a)). The other is the use of metal complexes of OSMs, which is realized with the help of the strong coordination of metal ions with the phosphate group (Scheme 1(b)) [17–24]. Most of the designed chemosensors only make use of the triphosphate group in the recognition process and have ignored the participation of the base in the nucleotide. And therefore, there are very few of examples that have evaluated the selectivity of all nucleosides triphosphate including ATP, GTP, CTP, and UTP [12, 15, 25]. In fact, it is tough to discriminate a certain nucleoside triphosphate against other ones since they hold the same triphosphate group and structurally planar bases, but it is essential for us to design such sensors in order to understand their respective functions. Although phenanthroline-containing polyammonium receptor [15] and pincer-like sensor with a pyrene excimer showed high selectivity for ATP among GTP, CTP, and UTP [12], both of them have to undertake complicated synthesis and separation steps. Therefore, finding new strategies to design and prepare new chemosensors for ATP with simple synthesis is still attractive and essential.
Herein, we developed another strategy by using metal organic frameworks (MOFs) based on the $\pi\cdot\pi$ stacking and the coordination of metal ions with both adenine and the phosphate group (Scheme 1(c)). The highlight of this strategy is that it takes into account the participation of the base in the recognition of ATP, which is a key factor in ensuring the good selectivity among the other nucleotides. MOFs have emerged as a new class of multifunctional materials in the past two decades, which have potential applications in sorption and separation, catalysis, biomedical imaging and drug delivery [26, 27]. Recently, MOFs as chemical sensors have increasingly developed for metal ions [28, 29], small molecules [30–32] and anions [33–35] such as $\mathrm{CN}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{CO_{3}}^{2-}$ and $\mathrm{F^{-}}$ . And luminescent MOFs are by far the most popular type of MOF sensor [36]. Whereas, using MOFs as chemosensors for recognition of nucleosides triphosphate such as ATP has rarely been reported. Our group [37] has reported that a luminescent terbium(III)-organic framework can be applied to highly selective sensing of CTP against other triphosphate nucleosides including ATP, GTP and UTP, but the disadvantage is that the system contained an organic solvent of DMF. Here, by introducing an easily synthesized luminescent MOF of $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ $\mathbf{\sigma}_{\mathrm{BDC}}=$ 1,4-benzenedicarboxylate) [38, 39], ATP could be effectively recognized and discriminated from other nucleosides triphosphate including GTP, CTP, and UTP in an aqueous solution, since the experiment showed that only ATP can induce the unique redshift of the emission of the luminescent MOF.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Chemicals and material
Adenosine $5^{\prime}{\mathrm{.}}$ -triphosphate disodium salt (ATP), cytidine $5^{\prime}$ -triphosphate disodium salt hydrate (CTP), uridine $5^{\prime}$ -triphosphate trisodium salt hydrate (UTP), guanosine $5^{\prime}$ -triphosphate disodium salt hydrate (GTP), adenosine ${5}^{\prime}$ -diphosphate sodium salt (ADP), and adenosine $5^{\prime}$ -monophosphate sodium salt (AMP) were all purchased from Sigma. Zinc nitrate hexahydrate was purchased from Boyi Chemical Co., Ltd (Chongqing, China). $p$ -Phthalic acid was purchased from Aladdin (Shanghai, China). Disodium Terephthalate was obtained from Tokyo Chemical Industry Co., Ltd. The sample of ATP injection $(20\mathrm{mg}:2\mathrm{mL})$ ) was purchased from a hospital in Chongqing. The other inorganic salts were obtained from commercial sources in China. Tris-HCl $(0.1\mathrm{\mol/L})$ was used as the buffer to adjust the acidity of the system. All reagents were of analytical grade without further purification. Doubly distilled water (18.2 M) was used throughout.
## 2.2 Instrumentation
Powder $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns were collected on an XD-3 X-ray diffractometer (Beijng, China) using $\mathrm{cu}$ $\mathrm{Ka}$ radiation $(\lambda=1.5406\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ). The scanning electron microscope (SEM) images were observed with a Hitachi S-4800 scanning electron microscope (Tokyo, Japan). The fluorescence spectra were measured on an F-4600 spectrophotometer keeping the excitation wavelength at $315.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ . $\mathrm{^1H}$ NMR and $^{31}\mathrm{P}$ NMR spectra in ${\bf D}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ were measured with AVANCE AV-300 (BRUKER, Swiss). QL-901 vortex mixer (Qilinbeier Instrument Manufacture Ltd., Haimen, China) was used to blend the solutions. BiLon ultrasonic cleaning machine (Shanghai, China) was used to disperse the MOF. ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms of the samples were obtained on a surface area analyzer (Quantachrome Autosorb-1, USA) at $77~\mathrm{K}$ . The samples were outgassed at room temperature under a pressure of ${{10}^{-3}}$ Torr for at least 5 h. Raman spectra were taken with HORIBA LabRAM HR $(785\mathrm{nm})$ .
## 2.3  Preparation of $\mathbf{\left[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}\right]}_{n}$
$\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ was synthesized according to the reference with a few modifications [39]. Simply, $p$ -phthalic acid 1 $\mathrm{\bf~\cdot~}\mathrm{\bf~H}_{2}\mathrm{\bfBDC}$ , $0.667~\mathrm{mmol}_{.}$ ) was dissolved in the aqueous solution $(5.0~\mathrm{mL})$ ) of $\mathrm{\DeltaNaOH}$ , which was adjusted to be neutral with an aqueous solution of hydrochloric acid. The obtained solution was mixed with an aqueous solution $(5.0~\mathrm{mL})$ of zinc nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\cdot6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $2.0\ \mathrm{mmol}\cdot$ ), accompanied with the rapid appearance of white precipitates. Further stirring for half an hour, the mixture was filtered, and the obtained solid was washed with distilled water and ethanol giving a white microcrystalline powder.
2.4  Pretreatment of the $\scriptstyle{[{\mathbf{Z}}\mathbf{n}(\mathbf{B}\mathbf{D}\mathbf{C})(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})_{2}]_{n}}$
The MOF of $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ was dispersed in distilled water with the help of ultrasonication for about $90~\mathrm{min}$ at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , $40\mathrm{kHz}$ . The dispersion system containing $0.1\ \mathrm{g/L}$ of $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ was obtained for further use.
## 2.5  Procedures of fluorescence titration with anions
$100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ Tris-HCl buffer solution and a certain volume of an anion solution were mixed together at first in a $1.5~\mathrm{mL}$ plastic tube. Afterwards, an appropriate volume of distilled water was added and mixed. At last, a certain volume of the $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ dispersion was added into the mixture and the final total volume was $1000~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ . After mixing thoroughly, the mixture was allowed to stand for $10~\mathrm{min}$ and then was transferred for fluorescence measurements.
## 3  Results and discussion
## 3.1  Spectral characteristics
$\mathrm{\Delta}[Z_{\mathrm{n}}(\mathrm{BDC})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]_{n}$ was synthesized according to a reference with a few modifications [39]. The phase purity of the obtained $\mathrm{\Delta}[Z{\mathrm{n}}(\mathrm{BDC})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]_{n}$ was confirmed by PXRD, and the sharp diffraction peaks in the PXRD pattern indicated that the MOF is highly crystalline (Supporting Information, Figure S1), which was also confirmed by the SEM image (Supporting Information, Figure S2). In $\mathrm{\Delta[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]_{\it n}},$ each $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ ion is coordinated by four oxygen atoms from the carboxylate groups of BDC and two water molecules [38, 39], and the presence of intermolecular hydrogen bonding results in a high-dimensional network [39]. When dispersed in water, $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ exhibits strong fluorescence emission at $427.6~\mathrm{nm}$ upon the excitation of 315.0 nm. In the presence of ATP, however, the emission shifts to $438.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 1(A)). As we noted, ATP alone and $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ -ATP mixture exhibit weak fluorescence centered at around $400.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 1(B), the inset), and ATP can greatly enhance the fluorescence emission of $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ at $380.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ upon the excitation of $315.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 1(B)). However, these emissions are greatly different from that of the ATP-induced red shift of $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ .
## 3.2  Response conditions of $\mathbf{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ to ATP
The effects of $\mathrm{\pH}$ values and ionic strength such as $\mathrm{\DeltaNaCl}$ were investigated on the redshift of the emission wavelength of $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ induced by ATP. As shown in
Figure 2(a), increasing $\mathrm{\pH}$ values from 7.2 to 9.0 has no effect on the emission wavelength of $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ . However, in the presence of ATP, the increasing $\mathrm{\pH}$ values will gradually prevent the redshift of the emission wavelength of $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ . Since ATP is an important biomolecule, it should be active at the physiological $\mathrm{\pH}$ value. When the $\mathrm{\pH}$ value is more than 8.0, ATP will gradually lose its activity, which is unfavourable to the interaction between $\mathrm{\Delta}[Z{\mathrm{n}}(\mathrm{BDC})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]_{n}$ and ATP. In order to maintain the activity of ATP, the physiological $\mathrm{\pH}$ value of 7.4 is used throughout the reaction. Meanwhile, the content of $\mathrm{{NaCl}}$ investigated in the range of $0.05{\mathrm{-}}0.3~{\mathrm{mol/L}}$ has no shielding effects on the redshift of the emission wavelength of $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ (Figure 2(b)), which means that the electrostatic interaction isn’t the dominating binding affinity between $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ and ATP. So, ionic strength has little effect on the recognition process and no addition of $\mathrm{\DeltaNaCl}$ is necessary to adjust the ionic strength.
3.3  Specific response of $\scriptstyle{[{\mathbf{Z}}\mathbf{n}(\mathbf{B}\mathbf{D}\mathbf{C})(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})_{2}]_{n}}$ to ATP
The ATP-induced redshift emission response of $[Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{BDC})\cdot$ - $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]_{n}$ is highly specific. In order to evaluate the specific response, other nucleosides triphosphate including GTP, CTP, and UTP as well as some inorganic anions were investigated. The experimental results show that no significant redshift in the emission wavelength of $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ is observed in the presence of these anions, even if $\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{4-}$ or ${\mathrm{PO}_{4}}^{3-}$ is added, demonstrating the high recognition selectivity of $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ for ATP (Figure 3(a) and Supporting Information, Figure S3). Since ATP, GTP, CTP, and UTP contain the same triphosphate group, it is likely that the adenine base plays an important role in the specific recognition system. Further, we test the fluorescence spectral changes of $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ upon addition of ADP and AMP. As illustrated in Figure 3(b), ADP induces the similar redshift in the emission wavelength as ATP does, but the extent is smaller. AMP has scarcely influence on the emission of $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ . This spectral difference caused by ATP, ADP and AMP indicates that both the adenine and the phosphate group are responsible for the recognition process.
## 3.4  Binding mode of $\mathbf{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ and ATP
${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption experiment was performed in order to evaluate the textural properties of $\mathrm{\Delta}[Z{\mathrm{n}}(\mathrm{BDC})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]_{n}.$ and the adsorption isotherm showed that almost no $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ was adsorbed at $77~\mathrm{K}$ despite the stable framework (Supporting Information, Figure S4), which indicates that $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ has too small pores to fit ${\bf N}_{2}$ to diffuse through its pores [40–42]. On the other hand, ATP, as we calculated in vacuum using the B3LYP functional [43] with 6-31G (d) basis set, has the volume of $0.48~\mathrm{nm}^{3}$ , which is larger than $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ whose volume is $0.043~\mathrm{nm}^{3}$ , and therefore it is difficult for ATP to get into the pores of $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ . Owing to the good dispersible character of $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ , ATP is able to be adsorbed on the surface of the structure of $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ .
Next, $\mathrm{^1H}$ NMR spectra were performed in ${\bf D}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in order to further investigate the interactions between them. BDC can cause upfield shifts of the H-2, H-8 of ATP in the $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR data, indicating there are $\pi-\pi$ stacking interactions between adenine and benzene in BDC (Figure 4) [14–15, 18, 44]. On the other hand, the coordination of $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ with ATP induces downfield shifts of the H-2, H-8 of ATP. However, ATP binding with $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ gives a downfield chemical shift of the H-8, and a much larger upfield shift of the H-2 than that in BDC/ATP system. It has been reported that $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ can interact with the N-7 in the adenine of ATP at high $\mathrm{pH}$ values of around 7.0 [45, 46]. So, besides the $\pi-\pi$ stacking interactions of $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ and ATP, the N-7 of ATP participates in the complexation with $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ from $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ at the $\mathrm{\pH}$ value of 7.4.
The binding mode of the phosphate group can be understood by analyzing $\ensuremath{{}^{31}\mathrm{P}}$ NMR spectra. The signals of $\mathbf{P}_{\upbeta}$ and $\mathbf{P}_{\gamma}$ phosphorus atoms in the phosphate chain of ATP are observed to display remarkable downfield shifts in the presence of the MOF of $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ , while the shift of the $\mathrm{P_{\alpha}}$ phosphorus atom to downfield is much smaller (Figure 5). Similar shifts have been reported before [15, 17, 20], indicating that in the process of interaction with $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ , the ${\upbeta}\mathrm{.}$ - and $\gamma\cdot$ -phosphates of ATP play a predominant role. Meanwhile, a relative insignificant downfield shifts in $\mathbf{P}_{\upbeta}$ and $\mathrm{P_{\alpha}}$ signals of ADP are observed when $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ is added, and there is almost no such shift for AMP (Supporting Information, Figure S5 and S6). The downfield shifts of the $\mathrm{^{31}P}$ signals in the phosphate chain of ATP, ADP and AMP are corresponding to the redshift of the emission spectra of $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ induced by them with the trend of $\mathrm{ATP}>\mathrm{ADP}>\mathrm{AMP}$ , which is obviously attributed to the number of the phosphate group.
Also, we compared the Raman signals of the $\mathrm{\Delta}[Z{\mathrm{n}}(\mathrm{BDC})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]_{n}$ before and after reacting with ATP since Raman spectroscopy is useful to monitor chemical and physical characteristics of the functional group. Two new bands appeared at 355 and $834~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , suggesting that new chemical binding species formed between $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ and ATP (Figure 6). Generally, the bands in the region of 150–450 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be assigned to the vibrations of metal-oxygen. So the band at $355~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ is attributed to the $Z\mathrm{n-O}$ vibration induced by the coordination of $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ and the phosphate group of ATP. Corresponding with the $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectra, the other vibration at $834~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ maybe caused by $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ binding to the N-7 in the adenine of ATP. In addition, XRD data showed that binding to ATP made the crystal structure of the MOF of $\mathrm{\Delta}[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{BDC})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]_{n}$ changed a little owing to the formation of new chemical bonds (Supporting Information, Figure S7).
## 3.5 Mechanism of the interaction between $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ and ATP
In such case, we can conclude that MOFs can be used for recognizing ATP based on the $\pi\cdot\pi$ stacking and the coordination of metal ions with both adenine and the phosphate group (Scheme 1(c)). In $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ , the ligand BDC is coordinated to $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ through the bis-monodentated coordination mode, forming a 1-D chain [39], and $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ as the metal center is four-coordinated. Both the N-7 and phosphate group of ATP can occupy the vacant coordination sites of $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ . Such coordination process could stabilize the excited state of the MOF, leading to a redshift of the emission spectra. It is worth mentioning that CTP can induce a much smaller redshift than ATP as shown in Figure 3(a), because the pyrimidone moiety is a weaker electron donor [20], and this makes a weak coordination of CTP to the $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ in $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ . Different from the other two strategies, adenine herein participates in the coordination interaction with $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ , which plays a pivotal role in the good selectivity for ATP among the other nucleosides triphosphate.
## 3.6  Detection of ATP in the sample of ATP injection
The redshift extent of the emission wavelength $(\Delta\lambda_{\mathrm{em}})$ of $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ is linearly correlated with the concentration of ATP in the range of $0.3{\mathrm{-}}1.8{\mathrm{~mmol/L}}$ $(\Delta\lambda_{\mathrm{em}}=-1.6+$ $5.6c_{\mathrm{ATP}}$ (mmol/L), $r=0.9942$ , from three measurements, as shown in Figure S8, Supporting Information). Owing to the near UV excitation and emission as well as the large size of the MOF particle of $\mathrm{\Delta[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}.$ this system cannot be applied to the recognition of ATP in biological samples such as cells. In order to test the analytical application of the present method, the sample of ATP injection was tested according to the general procedure. After dilution, the content of ATP in the sample of ATP injection was determined with the value of $0.43\pm0.02\ \mathrm{mmol/L}$ $(n=5)$ ), which was almost consistent with the value of $0.45\ \mathrm{mmol/L}$ obtained from transforming the labeled amount under the detection condition.
## 4 Conclusions
In conclusion, a facilely prepared metal organic framework (MOF) of $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ was introduced for selective fluorescent recognition and discrimination of ATP, especially among other nucleosides triphosphate including GTP,
CTP and UTP. $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ dispersed in water showed a remarkable redshift of the emission wavelength in the presence of ATP. The interaction between $\mathrm{[Zn(BDC)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ and ATP was confirmed by $\mathrm{^{1}H N M R}$ and $\ensuremath{{}^{31}\mathrm{P}}$ NMR spectra, involving both $\pi\cdot\pi$ stacking interactions and the coordination of $Z\mathrm{n(II)}$ with adenine and the phosphate group, which testified that the participation of the base in the recognition of nucleotides can improve the good selectivity among the other ones. To our knowledge, the application of luminescent MOFs to biological samples is still in its infancy. This unique sensing system for ATP with a luminescent MOF may promote the development of designing luminescent MOFs with near infrared excitation and emission as well as appropriate sizes for the selective sensing of a certain nucleoside triphosphate among the other ones in biological samples such as cells.
